  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Puhi’s name comes from a cave in Haʻikū Ahupuaʻa where the shark god Ka‑holi‑a‑Kāne once lived, marking the area as a wahi pana.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/puhi_hawaii",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 19th century, the Līhuʻe Plantation Company began sugar cultivation in Puhi in 1849, and Grove Farm later converted taro terraces into cane fields and rice paddies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/puhi_hawaii",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Around 1920, Grove Farm built Puhi Camp to house over 1,200 workers from Japan, the Philippines, China, Portugal, and Puerto Rico, with stores, a medical facility, and gardens.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/puhi_hawaii",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community is part of Kauaʻi County, one of Hawaii’s most geographically and culturally significant islands, and reflects modern development on the island.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
